==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*pekʷ-}}
From {{prefix|en|dys|}} {{der|en|grc|πέψις||digestion}} {{suffix|en||ia}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/dɪsˈpɛp.si.ə/|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vininn126-dyspepsia.wav|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|pathology}} Any [[mild]] [[disorder]] of [[digestion]] characterised by [[stomach]] pain, discomfort, [[heartburn]], and [[nausea]], often following a meal.
#: {{ux|en|He divides '''dyspepsias''' into accidental, temporary (indigestions) and habitual.}}
#* {{RQ:Landon Lady Anne Granard|page=253|volume=II|passage=It is not my strength of mind (exert it as I may) that will prevent me from experiencing '''dyspepsia''', if such follies as these are exhibited before me.}}
#* {{RQ:Doyle Land of Mist|passage="And the cramps when my body - like a badly articulated skeleton - would all get twisted into one rigid tangle! But now, except some '''dyspepsia''' and urticaria of the palms, I am free from pain."}}
#* {{RQ:Wodehouse Jeeves in the Offing|chapter=XII|passage=I've never hunted myself, but I understand that half the battle is being able to make noises like some jungle animal with '''dyspepsia''', and I believe that Aunt Dahlia in her prime could lift fellow-members of the Quorn and Pytchley out of their saddles with a single yip, though separated from them by two ploughed fields and a spinney.}}

